--TEST--
Core\Db::open asks `db.open` about the host a settings object wrote, and then puts the address it resolves to through the denied ranges in full — the check a `connect`-named endpoint is exempt from, `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities` and `rule:security/net-address-policy`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The host this deployment does not grant. The refusal is the capability's,
// and nothing here resolved a name or opened a socket to produce it: `db.open`
// names hosts where `db.connect` names blocks, which is the whole reason there
// are two grants.
try {
    var $far = Core\Db::open({
        driver: Core\Db\Driver::Postgres,
        host: "db.example.test",
        database: "shop",
        user: "app",
        password: "hunter2",
    });
    echo "opened db.example.test", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// And the host it does grant, which is the asymmetry stated as a contrast:
// this is the very address `Core\Db::connect` reaches with no `net` key
// written at all, and `open` still cannot have it. A program-supplied address
// stays subject to `rule:http-server/allow-url-pins-the-address`'s table, so the grant is not the last word --
// `net.internal` would be, and this deployment writes none.
try {
    var $near = Core\Db::open({
        driver: Core\Db\Driver::Postgres,
        host: "127.0.0.1",
        database: "shop",
        user: "app",
        password: "hunter2",
    });
    echo "opened 127.0.0.1", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// `rule:security/denial-is-a-runtime-error`: a denial is the world refusing a call, and the program is
// still running underneath it.
echo "still running", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# One host granted, and no `[capabilities.net]` at all -- so the second call
# above is past the grant and refused by the range table instead.
[capabilities.db]
open = ["127.0.0.1"]
--EXPECT--
Core\Db::open needs the capability `db.open` for db.example.test, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.db]`
Core\Db::open refuses 127.0.0.1: it is loopback (127.0.0.0/8), which `net.internal` does not except
still running
